# A Renaissance in Theatre
Tamil title: நாடகத்திலே மறுமலர்ச்சி

This 1954 address explains why a renaissance in theatre is of the utmost importance.

A renaissance is now occurring throughout many fields in the country. Stumbling as it goes, yet certainly moving forward, the country advances toward a new condition. Those who believe that upon attaining it our country can become one of the world's leading nations have engaged in this sacred work.

Today, whichever field one observes, confusion and agitation are visible. Some feel fear, many feel doubt, and only a very small minority possess clarity. It is true that a new condition is taking shape for the country.

When society's circumstances, thought and conduct become like a cracked field, a lamp about to go out, a tree continually withering, a vine drying up or a pond losing its water, a few people come forward with the desire to remove that misery, confidence that it can be removed and the intellectual ability to remove it.

The country does not welcome them as they labour in the farm of knowledge; it mocks them. It gives no respect, but showers them with envy. It offers no assistance, but causes trouble. Nevertheless, without ceasing their labour or becoming victims of weariness, anger, hatred and enmity, those few work with smiles mingled with sighs. They strive until the withered tree sprouts and the fading lamp shines again; they find victory and create a renaissance.

When what exists seems wrong, inadequate or useless, we correct, renew and make it useful. This society remains in a condition where it can be reconstructed and, if reconstructed, can yield both culture and benefit.

If Westerners successfully undertake the immense effort of using scientific instruments to descend to the seabed and recover sections and cargo from a ship shattered into a hundred pieces and submerged for years, why should it be impossible to renew a country and society that once lived in an exalted condition and held a high place in the world, but declined in an intervening age after falling into the grip of deceivers? This unyielding confidence is the great support of those who work in the renaissance movement.

If we closely examine the renaissance in theatre during the past ten years, the movement's speed and success become clear.

Theatre today is not noise at midnight. It is not mere dance and song attempting to delight the half-asleep through gait, costume, glance and speech. Theatre has courageously come forward as a good teacher to the country.

Today it is almost impossible to see on stage a Sita weeping in Ashoka Vana with a wristwatch upon her arm; a Kovalan arriving in a polo-collar shirt, gold-plated teeth, polished slippers and gold-framed spectacles; or a Hanuman who sings every line. One or two people preserve these relics of the old age at enormous expense, but in general the stage scenes seen ten years ago can no longer be seen today.

Today, the human being can be seen upon the stage, from many angles and in many conditions. This immense transformation has not remained within the theatrical world; through it, a great change of mind has occurred among the people.

Literature, music and drama must help people develop knowledge, awaken interest, create inner joy and show the good path.

Drama can secure these benefits particularly easily. In literature and music, one can only hear ideas explained. In drama, one can both hear the explanation and see scenes that illustrate it. Theatre can therefore take hold of the mind to a greater extent.

A good actor can instil in spectators at that moment the emotion he desires. Like electrified wires, the skill of a good actor makes the audience emotionally charged. Theatre thus possesses great persuasive power. Even an ordinary person with less knowledge than is needed to understand literature or appreciate music can understand theatre. It makes ideas intelligible, arouses emotion and prepares people for action. Such is the power of drama.

It is a fundamental truth that the threefold Tamil arts, and drama in particular, must serve good purposes: developing knowledge, awakening interest and allowing good qualities to take residence in the heart. Time does not alter this truth; if it is altered, only degeneration results.

The renaissance movement does not reject these fundamental truths. Renaissance is not a poison that destroys excellence; it is a method of destroying poisonous flowers that destroy excellence.

Drama must serve intellectual growth. The renaissance movement does not deny this. It accepts that knowledge must grow, but asks: what kind of knowledge?

“The world is illusion and the body impermanent”—this too is one kind of knowledge. “Do not trust a sari-clad woman”—that too is a kind of knowledge. “Wife and children are borrowed; even the mothers who bore you are borrowed”—this also belongs to one kind of knowledge.

What kind of knowledge should the stage provide? Ancient geography said there were fourteen worlds, seven below and seven above. Should theatre teach that even after the atlas has been published? After learning about the sun in science class, should one see as evidence upon the stage a Sun God driving his chariot?

Knowledge is indeed necessary, and enabling knowledge to grow is one purpose of theatre. The renaissance movement does not conceal this. But when theatre is used for intellectual growth, it says we must identify the kind of knowledge people need now and stage plays whose ideas and visual arrangements provide it.

Before efforts at renaissance began, the ideas implanted in people's minds through theatre were of many kinds. Yet if we separate, collect and sift them, we find that they conveyed three principal ideas:

Fate.

Life in the world above.

A separate justice for each caste.

What explanation did the stage, through story and spectacle, offer for people's rise and fall, happiness and anxiety, birth and death, wealthy living and a life eaten away by poverty, delight dancing in mansions and suffering poured into huts? Fate. “Whom has fate spared? See whom destiny has left untouched,” actor Ramachandra would sing.

Before him, in the guise of a bark-clad Sita Devi, the spectator is a mill worker who has laboured until his chest aches, daubed his forehead with vermilion from the Mahakali temple and flies desperately for half a stomach's food.

Before the labourer who sweats into the soil, suffers with his hand as pillow, hard ground as bed and empty stomach as companion, what knowledge can be cultivated by displaying Satyavan who suffered as a woodcutter, Harischandra who lost his kingdom and guarded the cremation ground, and Nallathangal who cast her children into a well? It can only make him sob, “Fate, fate.”

After this scorpion sting, he receives a drop of honey: a vision of the upper world, with the wish-fulfilling tree and Kamadhenu. This is not the knowledge needed today.

Likewise, the victorious king is shown kneeling before the guru. What does it signify?

Fifteen years ago, the stage was a royal court. Its preaching of caste order would be explicit:

“Do the Brahmins conduct worship in the temples?”

“Yes, Your Majesty. Worship takes place six times a day.”

“Are the Vaishyas engaged in increasing wealth?”

“Yes, Your Majesty. The Vaishyas are engaged in trade.”

“Do the Shudras perform their duties?”

“Yes, Your Majesty.”

That was royal-court dialogue. The guru blessing the king would advise him to rule without departing from Manu's law.

This is not merely an age that rejects Manu; it is an age that has sent kings home. Should the stage not change accordingly? That change is renaissance.

Plays customarily contained the three ideas of fate, the upper world and separate justice for each caste. Between them came the suffering of a chaste wife and God removing it, a trial for a devotee and the Lord finally appearing before him.

The renaissance movement recognised that the ideas traditionally supplied through theatre are unnecessary today, produce evil, lack justice and honesty, and injure the common welfare of society. It said the stage must provide new thoughts—ideas needed for a new world—and it has achieved notable success.

Anyone examining fifteen years of theatrical history will discover an astonishing truth. During these fifteen years, no more than one or two new mythological plays have been staged. This is not for want of myths; the books contain enough mythological stories for two generations. Yet they have not reached the stage.

Instead, new plays such as *Manithan*, *Inspector*, *Velaikkari*, *Velaikkaran*, *Vaazha Mudiyathavargal*, *Mullil Roja*, *Manamagal*, *Kaithi*, *Andaman Kaithi*, *Ethirparathathu*, *Balipeedam*, *Thozhilali*, *Siraichalai* and *Marumanam* are staged. All are modern plays portraying human nature and circumstance.

Ten years ago, a chaste woman had to die in order for a play to illustrate her virtue. Today, when a woman has gone astray, the husband sympathetically investigates why—and it happens in a play. I do not contrast a mythological play with a social play. Both examples are social plays, produced by the same theatrical company.

Ten years ago, a story had to end with the chaste woman dying before it could be staged; audiences could digest nothing beyond that. I remember well discussing that story with an actor friend. I asked why the woman had to die. Let her live, I said; let her live and teach a lesson to the scoundrel who did not know how to value love. Why not tell the story in that way? The question was not whether the country would accept it; the actor himself did not dare accept it.

Ten years later, the same company obtained the opportunity to portray the sorrowful story of a woman struck by circumstance. Now the conclusion is not death but forgiveness—and forgiveness from her husband, not because he is a god, but because he is human.

Such an immense change has occurred upon the stage.

Do not imagine I am saying that renaissance means forgiving every wife who errs and every friend who betrays. I ask you to consider what it means when plays that show such people and family events, and explain their social circumstances, are now staged and receive immense public support.

People wish to see the human being upon the stage. Their eyes and minds have grown weary of seeing God, sage, king, magician, avatars and devotees. Some ideas in those stories have soured; some have proved false; some have become objects of ridicule before the world; most do not suit practical life. They neither explain life's problems nor resolve its entanglements.

People now want to see how a human being like themselves changes under different circumstances, what he does and what he thinks, and they want to know the causes.

Eyes that once saw a skeleton become a woman now wish to see the cruelties within a family that reduce a woman to a skeleton. They wish to see a man who has lost his sight: one blinded by poverty or another's cruelty for the sake of a friend, family, country or some other cause. They want to see the worker worn down by labour and the man who has learned to live without working; the royal way of displaying upper-class life; the hunted person; the person whose frustrated desire has left him in pain; and one who will do anything to succeed in life.

They wish to see the relationship between father and son and between elder and younger brother, the disturbances that enter those relationships and their causes. They wish to see the circumstances that turn a formerly good person bad. Along with these things, they wish to know how everyone may live happily without exploiting one another and how the human being may avoid becoming an animal.

No one denies that this society is a ruined building. No intellectual fails to say it must be renewed. Yet there still are people in this country today who call plays explaining social conditions, caste hierarchy and its disasters incitements to caste and communal hatred; who say plays exposing the harms of superstition, the suffering inflicted upon ordinary people by frauds and spreading rationalism propagate atheism; and who call a play explaining that terrible economic inequality divides society, that life is everyone's right and that institutions depriving people of it must be destroyed an act of worship to the communist revolution. Through such people, obstacles are placed before the theatrical renaissance.

It is ignorance to fear that communalism, atheism and communist revolution will be introduced through drama. If the enemies of renaissance truly possess that fear, then class injustices and divisions will certainly disappear. The poisonous growth will be destroyed—is that a loss? Folly will be defeated and its roots broken. A path will be found for everyone to attain happiness. In the economic sphere, is that not worthy of praise?

With this understanding and courage of heart, those who govern our towns must support and encourage the renaissance taking shape in theatre. Contrary to the unrest stirred by people whose ideas lack clarity, the renaissance blossoming in theatre will not produce immorality. It will clarify what morality is and help it grow. It will not destroy justice or honesty; it will identify what justice is and establish it.

Jawaharlal said sorrowfully in Bangalore last month: “Because people fought in ancient times with bow and arrow, can those weapons be given to the army today? Or can I travel from Bangalore to Delhi in a bullock cart according to ancient custom? No one would propose such things. Yet in social questions, many among us still possess a bullock-cart mentality.”

We enjoy every facility of twentieth-century life without omitting one. Is it fitting for our social organisation to remain suited to the age of Vikramaditya? Is an aeroplane meant to carry a goat?

Society needs new methods and a new transformation in its organisation. The lofty work of creating that new form is renaissance. Theatre is a good instrument for making the great majority understand its necessity.

Therefore, a renaissance in theatre is of the utmost importance. It is a good measure of the country's awakening and a sign of what we shall be tomorrow.

---NOTES---
The speech dates itself to 1954 and surveys changes over the preceding ten to fifteen years. Named plays are retained in transliteration because they function as a historical catalogue rather than as generic descriptions.

The source phrase OCRed as “மா உரிதரித்த வேடத்தில் சீதா” is contextually read as “மரவுரி தரித்த வேடத்தில் சீதா” (Sita dressed in bark cloth). The Tamil source remains unchanged.

The sentence “எலும்பைப் பெண்ணுருவாகக் கண்ட கண்கள்...” alludes to a miraculous skeleton becoming a woman, then reverses the image to demand attention to social cruelty that turns a living woman skeletal.

Historical caste terminology and the speech's descriptions of older drama are translated in context without endorsing or silently modernising them.
